course: biology
lesson: 280
title: Evolution myths put right: monkeys, the strongest and missing links
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: «People came from monkeys» is a myth: we did not come from today's monkeys or chimps, but we share ancestors with them, the way you and your cousin share grandparents without one of you coming from the other.
explain: The famous picture of a monkey walking step by step into a man is misleading too, because evolution is like a bushy tree with many branches, and several kinds of human relatives lived at the same time.
explain: «Survival of the fittest» does not mean the strongest wins: «fittest» means best fitted to the place, so a tiny mouse, which needs little food and can hide, can do better than a huge mammoth when the climate changes.
explain: Animals also cannot choose to evolve: changes in DNA happen by chance, and the place decides which ones last, so the giraffe never wanted a long neck.
explain: And Pokémon do not evolve in the science sense: one creature changing in its own life is growing up, like a caterpillar becoming a butterfly, while evolution happens to whole groups over many generations.
fact: People did not come from today's monkeys or chimps; we share common ancestors with them, and chimps have been evolving for exactly as long as we have.
fact: «Fittest» means best fitted to the place, not the strongest; helping others can also be fit, as ants and wolves show.
fact: The famous picture of a line of apes turning into a man is misleading: evolution is a branching bush, not a ladder, and many human relatives lived at the same time.
fact: In science a theory is a big explanation backed by a mountain of evidence, like the theory of gravity; in everyday talk «theory» means a guess, but in science it does not.
fact: Evolution is supported by fossils, by DNA and by changes we can watch happening today.
fact: Humans are still evolving, slowly; the spread of milk digestion and resistance to some diseases are examples, over thousands of years, not one lifetime.
fact: An animal cannot choose to evolve; changes happen by chance in DNA, and the place decides which ones last.
fact: Thousands of in-between fossils are known, such as Tiktaalik, Archaeopteryx and early whales; scientists joke that every new fossil fills one gap but makes two smaller ones.
fact: When a Pokémon evolves, one creature changes in its life, which is like metamorphosis or growing up, not evolution; real evolution happens to groups over generations.
fact: Many religious people and scientists accept evolution and also have faith; science explains how living things change, faith answers other questions for believers, and people answer this in different ways and should respect each other.
fact: The tailbone, or coccyx, is what is left of a tail, and it still anchors muscles; such leftover parts are called vestigial, and they may have new jobs.
fact: Goosebumps are left from ancestors with fur: tiny muscles fluffed the fur up to keep warm or look bigger.
fact: Neanderthals made tools, used fire, buried their dead and maybe made art; their brains were as big as ours, and they had children with our ancestors.
exercise: Did people come from today's monkeys or chimps? | No, we share common ancestors with them, like cousins | Yes, from chimps in Africa | Yes, from the small, clever monkeys of South America | No, people came from fish just last year
exercise: What does «fittest» mean in «survival of the fittest»? | Best fitted to the place it lives | The strongest in a fight | The fastest runner | The one with the biggest body
exercise: What shape is evolution better compared to? | A branching bush | A straight ladder | A single straight line | A closed circle that loops
exercise: What does «theory» mean in science? | A big explanation backed by lots of evidence | A wild guess that someone makes up one day | Something nobody believes | A story for children
exercise: Which is another well-known scientific theory? | The theory of gravity | The theory of tooth fairies | The theory of good luck | The theory of the flat Earth
exercise: Which evidence supports evolution? | Fossils, DNA and changes we can watch today | Only old books | Only dreams people have | Only what a famous king said long ago
exercise: Are humans still evolving? | Yes, slowly, over thousands of years | No, evolution stopped thousands of years ago | Yes, each person changes into a new species | No, only wild animals and plants evolve
exercise: Can an animal choose to evolve? | No, changes happen by chance and the place decides which last | Yes, if it really wants to and wishes hard every day of its life | Yes, but only clever animals | Yes, by training hard
exercise: Which of these is an in-between fossil? | Archaeopteryx | A modern pigeon | A tabby house cat | A goldfish in a bowl
exercise: What is the scientists' joke about missing links? | Every new fossil fills one gap but makes two smaller ones | Every missing link is hiding in a museum cupboard somewhere | Missing links only appear on Tuesdays | Every fossil is a missing link to the Moon
exercise: When a Pokémon «evolves», what is it most like in real life? | Growing up, like a caterpillar becoming a butterfly | Real evolution of a whole species over millions of years | A lizard losing its tail | A dog learning a trick
exercise: Can a person believe in God and accept evolution? | Yes, many religious people and scientists do | No, it is against the law | No, scientists are never allowed to have faith | Only if they live in a church
exercise: What is the tailbone, or coccyx? | What is left of a tail, still anchoring muscles | A spare bone that helps you grow taller as a teenager | A bone that will grow into a tail | A bone that does nothing at all
exercise: What are goosebumps left over from? | Ancestors with fur that fluffed up | Ancestors that had feathers like geese | Ancestors covered in hard, shiny scales | Ancestors who lived only in snow and ice
exercise: What could Neanderthals do? | Make tools, use fire and bury their dead | Only grunt and hit things | Nothing, they lived just like apes in the trees | Build cars and houses
exercise: How big were Neanderthal brains? | About as big as ours | Half the size of ours | The size of a small walnut | Ten times bigger than ours
test: «we came from monkeys, so why are there still monkeys, checkmate» What is the right answer? | We did not come from today's monkeys; we share ancestors, like cousins | The monkeys are waiting their turn to turn into people, one by one | Monkeys came from people instead | There will be no monkeys soon
test: In a cartoon, a big strong lion is always the winner because it is «the fittest». What does «fittest» really mean? | Best fitted to its place, which may be a small or gentle animal | The one with the biggest muscles and the loudest roar in the land | The one who wins the most fights | The one that eats the most
test: The climate turns very cold and food is scarce. Who is more likely to survive: a huge mammoth or a tiny mouse? | The mouse, because it needs little food and can hide | The mammoth, because the biggest and strongest always win | Neither can survive any change | Both always survive the same
test: «it's just a theory, so it's basically a guess» What is right? | No, in science a theory is an explanation backed by a mountain of evidence | Yes, a theory is just a guess in science too, until it is proved and becomes a fact | Yes, like gravity is just a guess | No, a theory is a law of the government
test: In Pokémon, Charmander evolves into Charmeleon in one fight. What would a biologist say? | That is one creature changing, like growing up, not evolution | That is exactly how real evolution works, one big change at a time | Real lizards do this every week | Only fire animals can evolve fast
test: «a giraffe that really wanted a long neck would get one» What is right? | No, changes happen by chance, and the place decides which last | Yes, wishing hard makes the neck grow a little more every year | Yes, if it eats more leaves | No, giraffes never change at all
test: Count them: a museum shows Tiktaalik with 4 leg-like fins, Archaeopteryx with 2 legs and 2 wings, and an early whale with 4 legs. How many limbs in all? | 12 | 10 | 8 | 14
test: «that poster shows a monkey turning into a man in 5 steps, so it's a ladder» What is right? | The poster is misleading; evolution is a branching bush with many relatives at once | Yes, each step was one monkey's child, born looking a bit more like a person each time | Yes, and the man is the top of the ladder | No, the steps go the other way
test: «Neanderthals were thick cavemen with clubs» What is right? | They made tools, used fire, buried their dead and had brains as big as ours | Yes, they could not make anything at all and lived by hitting animals with sticks | Yes, they were a kind of gorilla | No, they were robots
test: «my tailbone's useless, docs could just take it out» What is closer to the truth? | It is left from a tail but still anchors muscles | It has no job at all | It is the place where a new tail will grow one day | It holds your spine up alone
test: «scientists are still missing all the links, it's all gaps» What is right? | Thousands of in-between fossils are known, like Tiktaalik and Archaeopteryx | No in-between fossils have ever been found, so there really is nothing but gaps | Every fossil ever found is the same animal | Fossils prove animals never changed
test: «my nan's religious, so she can't believe in evolution» What is best? | Many religious people accept evolution and keep their faith; people answer this in different ways | All religious people reject evolution, so your nan has to as well, whatever she thinks | Evolution is a religion | Nobody may talk about it
exam: Identify the meaning of the term «fittest» as employed in the expression «survival of the fittest». | Best suited to the conditions of the environment | Possessing the greatest physical strength | Possessing the greatest body size | Possessing the greatest speed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relationship between humans and present-day chimpanzees. | They share common ancestors, neither having descended from the other | Humans are descended directly from the chimpanzees living at the present day | Chimpanzees are descended from present-day humans | No relationship of any kind exists between them
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the meaning of the word «theory» in the sciences? | A comprehensive explanation supported by a substantial body of evidence | A conjecture unsupported by any evidence, awaiting confirmation by experiment | An opinion held by a single scientist | A rule enacted by the government
exam: State which of the following constitutes an example of continuing human evolution. | The spread of the capacity to digest milk in adulthood | The growth of muscle through exercise in one lifetime | The learning of a second language | The increase in height of a child as it grows
